findings.body:
The new guard returns before the base teardown on iPad and for `testSelectBottomPlacement`, which is every run on iPad and every run of that test on iPhone.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="27-38"}

The `return` at line 31 skips `app.terminate()` and `try await super.tearDown()` at lines 36-37. `BaseTestCase.tearDown` (line 119) does the same two steps, so the app keeps running into the next test, and XCTest's required `super.tearDown()` is never called. The theme-reset comment only justifies skipping the theme change, not the teardown. Restructure the method so the theme reset is conditional and the cleanup always runs.

Before this PR, the Light-theme reset ran on every device on iOS 17+. The `|| iPad()` condition now disables it for the whole class on iPad.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="28-35"}

`testFirstRunTourDarkMode` (lines 148-155) sets the theme to Dark and relies on `tearDown` to restore Light. It has no iPad skip, so on iPad it leaves the app in Dark mode. Later tests that assume Light, such as the toolbar placement tests, can then fail depending on run order. The iPad check was meant to skip only the toolbar-specific path, not the theme reset. Keep the reset unconditional on iPad, or at least for tests that changed the theme.

XCTest only runs instance methods whose names start with `test`. Renaming `testBookmarksShareNormalWebsiteReminders` to `verifyBookmarksShareNormalWebsiteReminders` removes the test from every run without a skip or a deletion.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/ShareLongPressTests.swift" lines="46-59"}

`AccessibilityTestPlan.xctestplan` line 371 still lists `ShareLongPressTests/testBookmarksShareNormalWebsiteReminders()`, which no longer matches any method. The result is a dangling plan entry and a silently lost check on the bookmarks-to-Reminders share flow, while the PR description says it was an attempt to fix flakiness. If the test is flaky, keep the `test` prefix and add a `throw XCTSkip` with a reason, or fix the flake. Renaming to hide it is not a fix.

The `self.name.contains("testSelectBottomPlacement")` clause is not needed. That test only changes toolbar placement and never calls `switchThemeToDarkOrLight`, so there is no theme state to skip. The comment on line 30 says the theme is not changed "for iPad", which does not describe a condition that also applies on iPhone. The name check also depends on the XCTest name string format, which is fragile.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="29-31"}

Remove the name check and keep a single, accurate iPad-only condition if it is still needed at all after the reset fix above.

`let shouldSkipTest = true` followed by `XCTSkipIf(shouldSkipTest, ...)` is an always-true condition wrapped in a constant. The file's previous code used the plain `throw XCTSkip(...)` pattern, which this PR removed from the same test.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="348-352"}

Moving the iPad check above the Terms of Service tap is the right fix. The mechanism can be simpler.

The new line taps "Allow Paste" only if it appears within 0.5 s. If the system prompt shows up later, the paste step proceeds without dismissing it, and the flake the PR targets remains. When no prompt appears, every paste call pays the full 0.5 s.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/BaseTestCase.swift" lines="468-473"}

I could not confirm from the code where this prompt is presented or how long it takes, so treat this as a risk to check, not a confirmed defect. If the prompt is a real intermittent blocker, a longer timeout (for example 2-3 s) or a wait on the prompt's appearance is more reliable than a fixed short tap window.

This is the part of the PR that most directly targets flakiness. The old code used a fixed `press(forDuration: 1.0)`, which could fire before the context menu was ready. The new code waits on the `Context Menu` table through `pressWithRetry`, which retries the press until the menu exists, then taps Share from that same table.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/ShareLongPressTests.swift" lines="246-249"}

Scoping `contextMenu.buttons["shareLarge"]` to the menu table also avoids matching a Share button elsewhere on screen.
